  "sections": [
    {
      "phase": "reference",
      "phase_label": "Aircraft Description",
      "title": "Aircraft Description",
      "criticality": "normal",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "General",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The Yak 50 is an all-metal tail dragger designed solely for advanced aerobatics training and competition.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The fuselage is semi monocoque, a combination of stressed skin and steel re-enforcement.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The strong but simple main gear is semi-retractable, the lockable tailwheel permanently extended.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The Yak 50 has no flaps.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Flying controls are conventional and operated by control cables for the elevator and rudder, push rods for the ailerons.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Only the elevator trim is adjustable in flight, the rudder and ailerons served by fixed tabs which are manipulated on the ground.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Engine Power",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Powerplant: Ivchenko M14 radial, supercharged nine cylinder radial, 360hp at max rpm.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Propeller: variable pitched two-bladed paddle via a simple gearbox.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 0.5
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Fuel capacity on the basic aircraft is 11.4 imperial gallons contained in a single tank mounted aft of the firewall.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 0.5
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Maximum flight endurance is quoted as 50 minutes and this figure can be halved during advanced aerobatic flights.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 0.5
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "A welcome mod fitted to the vast majority of Yak 50s is the installation of a cruise tank which more than doubles the fuel capacity, although the G limits are reduced when full fuel is carried.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The Yak 50 uses pneumatics for engine starting, gear and brake operation.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The aircraft's air bottle will give around 12 start attempts before a recharge is necessary and a reserve bottle is also installed for emergency gear extension.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The main bottle gets recharged automatically once the engine has started.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "subtitle",
          "text": "Cockpit",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The undercarriage lever is positioned on the left of the main panel, two green lights confirming that the gear is down and locked when the aircraft is on the ground.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The throttle, rpm and lock lever form a meaty quadrant on the left shelf with another panel ahead comprising levers for the engine cooling gills and oil cooler flap.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "For most of the year the cooling levers will reside in the fully forward (open) position.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The big red lever is the fuel shut off, replacing the emergency function of a mixture lever. Fuel mixture control is fully automatic.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The instrument panel uses metric units. Most owners replace the altimeter since it reads in meters. The airspeed indicator reads in km/h.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The engine has two main control indicators: an rpm gauge reading from 0 to 100%, and a manifold pressure gauge reading in mm of mercury.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The Yak 50's electrics are in the centre of the panel and the fuel primer is located to the top right.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "The mag switch is mounted on the left of the panel next to the engine starter button.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "phase": "preflight_inspection",
      "title": "Preflight / Before Entering Cockpit",
      "criticality": "normal",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Climbing into the cockpit and strapping into the chunky five point harness, one is immediately aware of the ruggedness of the Yak 50.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Visibility from the cockpit is excellent in every direction except forwards.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        }
      ]
