    {
      "phase": "reference",
      "phase_label": "Control Harmony and Aerobatics",
      "title": "Control Harmony and Aerobatics",
      "criticality": "normal",
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Control harmony is typically 3.2.3 for ailerons, elevator and rudder instead of the classical 1,2,3.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Spades are an option on the Yak 50, recommended for serious aerobatic pilots.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "In pitch the Yak 50's stick force per G is very light; pulling up to the nine G maximum does not require excessive strength.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "During advanced aerobatics many pilots release the throttle to fly with two hands on the stick.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "With the aircraft wound up to its Vno of 400 km/h (226kt) the Yak 50 can perform huge lazy loops, barrel rolls and wing overs with low G forces.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "Stalls and spins are surprisingly docile. Stall and spin recovery are predictable and standard. Some advanced aerobats have to be positively wrestled out of an established spin; the Yak 50 is one of the exceptions.",
          "tickable": false,
          "source_confidence": 1.0
        }
      ]
    },
